#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ctlhq-first-push: check a repository's history for personal data, a README, a LICENSE, and the push owner.

Usage:
  ctlhq-first-push [--new-only] [--json] [--root DIR] [--remote NAME]
  ctlhq-first-push hook install [--root DIR]
  ctlhq-first-push hook status [--root DIR]

Settings: ~/.ctlhq/first-push.toml (HOME is honoured).
  github_owner = "<your GitHub account>"   the only GitHub account a push may go to (OWNER
                                           below), matched in any letter case; a "#"
                                           comment may follow the value. Read only from
                                           ~/.ctlhq/first-push.toml, never from
                                           FIRST_PUSH_CONFIG, so all three push tools agree.
                                           ctlhq-push and ctlhq-preflight read the same
                                           line. Without it, a GitHub push target is a
                                           push.owner-not-configured finding.
  public_terms = ["term", ...]             terms that are not treated as private.
                                           FIRST_PUSH_CONFIG names another file to read
                                           this line from.

What it checks, in order:
  1. Policy. The private-values list (.local/first-commit/private-values.txt) must exist,
     hold at least one value, be ignored by .gitignore, and not be tracked. A missing list
     is a finding.
  2. Push target. A GitHub push address of origin (or --remote NAME) must be
     github.com/OWNER/... in any address form; another owner is a finding, and so is any
     GitHub address while github_owner is not configured. A non-GitHub address (another
     host, a local path, file://) or no such remote is not checked, and the report says so;
     the other checks still run and can still block.
  3. Every commit in range, oldest first: each added or changed text file, the message,
     and the author and committer name and email, with first-commit's personal-data rules
     (the same rules as first-commit's privacy check): configured private values, unapproved
     email addresses, home folder paths, private keys and token shapes, forbidden paths.
     Allowed: emails in public_emails of .repo-policy.json and the contributor record;
     GitHub no-reply addresses; example and reserved domains; content-matched, count-bound
     exceptions; public_github_owner_urls; immutable vendor files. gitleaks also scans the
     same range, taken from ~/.ctlhq/bin/gitleaks, else from the first absolute PATH
     folder that holds it; without it only the built-in token shapes run, and the report
     says so. A gitleaks inside the checked repository (such as
     .local/bin/gitleaks) is never run, because a repository could ship a program there.
     ~/.ctlhq/bin sits beside the settings file, outside every repository, and a pre-push
     hook started by a git app may not get your shell's PATH.
     Identity name and email are checked separately: an email in the allowed list (ignoring
     case) is not a finding, and neither is a name equal to the account part of an allowed
     GitHub no-reply email. A no-reply email outside the list allows no name.
  4. LICENSE, LICENSE.md, LICENSE.txt, or COPYING at the top level of every commit in range,
     the root commit included.
  5. A README at the top level of the tip (HEAD), not empty.

Range: by default the whole history (HEAD, all branches, all tags). --new-only checks only
commits not reachable from any remote-tracking ref; with no remote, the whole history.

Output: one numbered line per finding, "<n>. <commit> <where> <rule>", where <where> is
"<file>:<line>", "message:<line>", "identity:author", "identity:committer", or a policy
item. A finding seen again in later commits shows "(+N more commits)". Right under each
finding, a "fix:" line says why it matters and the exact action, with the file, line,
commit, or command filled in. Then "ready" or "not ready", then one "next:" line with the
next command (ready: "ctlhq-first-push hook install" while the hook is missing, then adding a
GitHub remote, then the push). No output ever holds a private value, an email address,
or the push address; a file path that holds a private value is shown as "<path redacted>".
--json prints one object: ready, mode, commits_checked, files_checked, gitleaks,
push_target, findings (each with a why and a fix field), next.

hook install adds a pre-push hook. A hook is ctlhq's own only when its
second line is the marker "# first-push hook v1", alone or followed by a colon.
Any other pre-push hook (for example the pre-commit framework's, set up by first-commit)
is the user's: it is kept as pre-push.before-first-push and runs first with the same
arguments and input; ctlhq-first-push runs after it.
A ctlhq hook is current while the tool path it records exists and can run, even when its
text differs (for example a hook written by another checkout). It is stale when that path is
gone or cannot run (for example an old .../bin/first-push), or when the hook file itself is
not executable. hook install moves a stale hook to pre-push.stale.<n> (the first free n),
prints that name, and writes a new hook; pre-push.before-first-push stays as it is. A
symbolic link at pre-push is never written through: unless it leads to a current ctlhq
hook, hook install changes nothing and exits 3. hook status prints
"installed", "stale: the hook <why>; ...", or "missing" (also when only the user's own
hook is there). For such a link, hook status and the next: line say to replace the link
with a copy of its target (or remove it) before hook install.

Rule encoded (AGENTS.md): "Keep blocked operations, working local actions, and missing
evidence separate in totals."

Exit 0 ready (hook status: installed), 1 findings (hook status: stale or missing), 2 usage error,
3 environment problem, so there is no verdict (not a git repository, no commits, an
unreadable or malformed policy or settings file, git or gitleaks failure, unsafe hook
folder). Exit 3 prints three lines on stderr: "ctlhq-first-push: <what is wrong>", "why: <why
there is no verdict>", "fix: <the action or command>". When the fix commits or changes a
hook, the third line starts "fix (operator decides):": the operator's yes comes first, so
an agent proposes it and does not run it. Paths under your home folder are
shown with ~, and no exit-3 line holds a private value or the content of a policy file.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import unicodedata
from collections import Counter
from pathlib import Path

OWNER_NAME = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9-]{0,38}")
GITLEAKS_FOLDER = Path(".ctlhq") / "bin"
PRIVATE_VALUES_FILE = ".local/first-commit/private-values.txt"
CONTRIBUTOR_PATH = ".local/first-commit/contributor.json"
LICENSE_NAMES = ("LICENSE", "LICENSE.md", "LICENSE.txt", "COPYING")
README = re.compile(r"(?i)readme(\.[^/]+)?")
HOOK_MARKER = "# first-push hook v1"
PREVIOUS_HOOK = "pre-push.before-first-push"
GITLEAKS_EXIT = 10
GITLEAKS_TIMEOUT = 300

# The rules below copy first-commit's privacy check (its EMAIL pattern and PATTERNS list).
EMAIL = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}")
PATTERNS = (
    ("privacy.private-key", re.compile(r"-----BEGIN (?:[A-Z0-9]+ )?PRIVATE KEY-----")),
    ("privacy.github-token", re.compile(r"gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}")),
    ("privacy.aws-access-key", re.compile(r"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}")),
    ("privacy.ssh-fingerprint", re.compile(r"SHA256:[A-Za-z0-9+/]{20,}={0,2}")),
    ("privacy.user-home-path", re.compile(r"(?:/Users|/home)/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+(?:/|\s|$)")),
)
EXAMPLE_DOMAINS = {"example.com", "example.net", "example.org"}
RESERVED_TEST_SUFFIXES = (".invalid", ".test")
PROTOCOL_DOMAINS = {"openssh.com", "libssh.org"}
GITHUB_NOREPLY = re.compile(r"(?i)(?:[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@users\.noreply\.github\.com|noreply@github\.com)")
NOREPLY_ACCOUNT = re.compile(r"(?:[0-9]+\+)?([a-z0-9._%-]+)@users\.noreply\.github\.com")
GITHUB_OWNER_PREFIX = re.compile(r"(?:https?://|ssh://git@|git@)github\.com[/:]$")
URL_FORMS = (
    re.compile(r"(?i)^(?:https?|git|ssh|git\+ssh)://(?:[^@/]+@)?(?P<host>[^/:]+)(?::\d*)?/+(?P<owner>[^/]+)/[^/]+"),
    re.compile(r"(?i)^(?:[^@/]+@)?(?P<host>[^/:]+):/*(?P<owner>[^/]+)/[^/]+"),
)


class EnvError(Exception):
    """An environment problem (exit 3): what is wrong, why there is no verdict, and the fix."""

    def __init__(self, what: str, why: str, fix: str, operator: bool = False) -> None:
        super().__init__(what)
        self.what, self.why, self.fix = what, why, fix
        # operator: the fix changes the repository (a commit, a hook), so the operator decides.
        self.label = "fix (operator decides)" if operator else "fix"


def shown(path) -> str:
    """A path for messages, with the home folder written as ~."""
    text, home = str(path), str(Path.home())
    if home not in ("", "/") and (text == home or text.startswith(home + os.sep)):
        return "~" + text[len(home):]
    return text


GIT_FIX = "Run `git status` in the repository to see what git reports, fix that, then run ctlhq-first-push again."


class UsageError(Exception):
    pass


def git(root: Path, *args: str, input_bytes: bytes | None = None, check: bool = True) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(root), "-c", "log.showSignature=false", "-c", "core.quotePath=false", *args],
            input=input_bytes, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, check=False,
        )
    except OSError as error:
        raise EnvError(f"git cannot be run ({error.strerror or error})",
                       "ctlhq-first-push reads the history with git.",
                       "Install git (on macOS: xcode-select --install), then run ctlhq-first-push again.") from error
    if check and proc.returncode != 0:
        raise EnvError(f"git {args[0]} failed (exit {proc.returncode})",
                       "ctlhq-first-push cannot give a verdict on history it could not read.", GIT_FIX)
    return proc


def git_text(root: Path, *args: str) -> str:
    return git(root, *args).stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- policy


class Policy:
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.allowed_emails: set = set()
        self.private_values: tuple = ()
        self.literal_exceptions: list = []  # (path, sha256)
        self.detector_exceptions: list = []  # (path, rule, sha256)
        self.forbidden_paths: list = []  # (match, value)
        self.vendor_hashes: dict = {}
        self.public_github_owner_urls = False
        self.findings: list = []  # (file, rule) policy-level findings


POLICY_WHY = ("ctlhq-first-push reads the allowed emails, the approved exceptions, and the forbidden paths "
              "from this file, so it cannot check history against the right rules.")


def read_json(path: Path, rel: str) -> dict:
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_bytes().decode("utf-8"))
    except OSError as error:
        raise EnvError(f"policy file {rel} cannot be read ({error.strerror or error})", POLICY_WHY,
                       f"Check its permission with `ls -l {rel}`, make it readable, then run ctlhq-first-push again.") from error
    except UnicodeDecodeError as error:
        raise EnvError(f"policy file {rel} is not UTF-8 text", POLICY_WHY,
                       f"Save {rel} as UTF-8, then run ctlhq-first-push again.") from error
    except json.JSONDecodeError as error:
        raise EnvError(f"policy file {rel} is not valid JSON (line {error.lineno}, column {error.colno}: {error.msg})",
                       POLICY_WHY,
                       f"Fix the JSON at line {error.lineno} of {rel} (`python3 -m json.tool {rel}` shows the error), "
                       "then run ctlhq-first-push again.") from error
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        raise EnvError(f"policy file {rel} is not a JSON object", POLICY_WHY,
                       f"Make the top level of {rel} a JSON object, such as {{\"schema_version\": 1}}, "
                       "then run ctlhq-first-push again.")
    return data


def entries(value, fields: tuple, label: str, rel: str) -> list:
    shape = "[{" + ", ".join(f'"{f}": "<{f}>"' for f in fields) + "}]"
    fix = f"Write {label} in {rel} as a JSON list of objects, each with text fields {', '.join(fields)}: {shape}."
    if value is None:
        return []
    if not isinstance(value, list):
        raise EnvError(f"policy field {label} in {rel} must be a list", POLICY_WHY, fix)
    out = []
    for n, item in enumerate(value, 1):
        if not isinstance(item, dict) or any(not isinstance(item.get(f), str) for f in fields):
            raise EnvError(f"policy field {label} in {rel} has an invalid entry (entry {n})", POLICY_WHY, fix)
        out.append(tuple(item[f] for f in fields))
    return out


def text_list(value, label: str, rel: str) -> list:
    if not isinstance(value, list) or not all(isinstance(e, str) for e in value):
        raise EnvError(f"policy field {label} in {rel} must be a list of text", POLICY_WHY,
                       f"Write {label} in {rel} as a JSON list of quoted addresses: [\"<address>\", ...].")
    return value


POLICY_FILE = ".repo-policy.json"


def load_policy(root: Path) -> Policy:
    policy = Policy()
    baseline = root / POLICY_FILE
    if baseline.exists():
        rel = POLICY_FILE
        data = read_json(baseline, rel)
        policy.allowed_emails.update(text_list(data.get("public_emails", []), "public_emails", rel))
        policy.literal_exceptions += entries(data.get("private_literal_exceptions"), ("path", "sha256"), "private_literal_exceptions", rel)
        policy.detector_exceptions += entries(data.get("privacy_detector_exceptions"), ("path", "rule", "sha256"), "privacy_detector_exceptions", rel)
        policy.forbidden_paths += entries(data.get("forbidden_paths"), ("match", "value"), "forbidden_paths", rel)
        policy.vendor_hashes.update(dict(entries(data.get("immutable_vendor_files"), ("path", "sha256"), "immutable_vendor_files", rel)))
        policy.public_github_owner_urls = data.get("public_github_owner_urls", False) is True
    contributor = root / CONTRIBUTOR_PATH
    if contributor.exists():
        email = read_json(contributor, CONTRIBUTOR_PATH).get("email")
        if isinstance(email, str):
            policy.allowed_emails.add(email)

    values: list = []
    rel = PRIVATE_VALUES_FILE
    path = root / rel
    if not path.is_symlink() and path.exists():
        try:
            text = path.read_bytes().decode("utf-8")
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
            raise EnvError(f"the private-values file {rel} is unreadable or not UTF-8 text",
                           "Without the list, ctlhq-first-push cannot look for your private values.",
                           "Save the list as UTF-8 text, one value per line, readable by you "
                           "(check with `ls -l` on the file), then run ctlhq-first-push again.") from error
        for line in text.splitlines():
            value = unicodedata.normalize("NFC", line.strip()).casefold()
            if value and not value.startswith("#") and value not in values:
                values.append(value)
        if git(root, "ls-files", "--cached", "--", rel).stdout.strip():
            policy.findings.append((rel, "policy.private-values-tracked"))
        elif git(root, "check-ignore", "-q", "--", rel, check=False).returncode != 0:
            policy.findings.append((rel, "policy.private-values-not-ignored"))
    if not values:
        policy.findings.append((rel, "policy.private-values-missing"))
    public = public_terms()
    policy.private_values = tuple(value for value in values if value not in public)
    return policy


def owner_file() -> Path:
    return Path.home() / ".ctlhq" / "first-push.toml"


def settings_file() -> Path:
    return Path(os.environ.get("FIRST_PUSH_CONFIG") or owner_file())


def owner_fix(path: Path) -> str:
    return f'Add the line github_owner = "<your GitHub account>" to {shown(path)}, then run this command again.'


def read_github_owner(path: Path) -> str | None:
    """The github_owner value, or None when the file or the line is missing.

    Raises ValueError when the file is unreadable or the value is not a GitHub account name in double quotes.
    """
    if not path.is_file():
        return None
    try:
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
        raise ValueError(f"{shown(path)} is unreadable or not UTF-8 text") from error
    line = re.search(r"(?m)^github_owner\s*=(.*)$", text)
    if not line:
        return None
    value = re.fullmatch(r'\s*"([^"]*)"\s*(?:#.*)?', line.group(1))
    if not value or not OWNER_NAME.fullmatch(value.group(1)):
        raise ValueError(f"github_owner in {shown(path)} is not a GitHub account name in double quotes")
    return value.group(1)


def configured_owner() -> str | None:
    path = owner_file()
    try:
        return read_github_owner(path)
    except ValueError as error:
        raise EnvError(str(error), "ctlhq-first-push stops rather than guess which GitHub account a push may go to.",
                       owner_fix(path)) from error


def public_terms() -> set:
    """Terms the operator declared public for ctlhq-first-push, from the settings file: a line
    `public_terms = ["term", ...]`. A missing file means no public terms. These terms are
    dropped from the private-values list before the check."""
    path = settings_file()
    if not path.is_file():
        return set()
    why = ("This file lists the terms you declared public; ctlhq-first-push stops rather than guess which "
           "terms are private.")
    shape = 'public_terms = ["term one", "term two"] (double quotes, commas between terms, all on one line)'
    try:
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
        raise EnvError(f"the public-terms file {shown(path)} is unreadable or not UTF-8 text", why,
                       f"Save {shown(path)} as UTF-8 text readable by you, with the line {shape}; "
                       "or point FIRST_PUSH_CONFIG at another file.") from error
    match = re.search(r"(?m)^public_terms\s*=\s*(\[.*?\])\s*$", text)
    if not match:
        return set()
    line = text.count("\n", 0, match.start()) + 1
    malformed = EnvError(f"public_terms in {shown(path)} line {line} is not a list of quoted strings", why,
                         f"Rewrite line {line} of {shown(path)} as {shape}.")
    try:
        terms = json.loads(match.group(1))
    except ValueError as error:
        raise malformed from error
    if not isinstance(terms, list) or not all(isinstance(term, str) for term in terms):
        raise malformed
    return {unicodedata.normalize("NFC", term.strip()).casefold() for term in terms if term.strip()}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- personal-data rules


def line_of(text: str, start: int) -> int:
    return text.count("\n", 0, start) + 1


def digest(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def approved_lines(raw_lines: list, budget: Counter) -> dict:
    """Lines whose exact text an exception approves; N exceptions approve the first N copies."""
    approved: dict = {}
    if not budget:
        return approved
    remaining = Counter(budget)
    wanted = {d for _, d in budget}
    for number, raw in enumerate(raw_lines, start=1):
        d = digest(raw)
        if d not in wanted:
            continue
        for key, key_digest in budget:
            if key_digest == d and remaining[(key, key_digest)] > 0:
                remaining[(key, key_digest)] -= 1
                approved.setdefault(key, set()).add(number)
    return approved


def email_allowed(address: str, policy: Policy) -> bool:
    if address in policy.allowed_emails or GITHUB_NOREPLY.fullmatch(address):
        return True
    domain = address.lower().rpartition("@")[2]
    return (
        domain in EXAMPLE_DOMAINS
        or domain in PROTOCOL_DOMAINS
        or domain.endswith(RESERVED_TEST_SUFFIXES)
    )


def github_owner(normalized: str, start: int, value: str) -> bool:
    end = start + len(value)
    if normalized[end:end + 1] != "/":
        return False
    return GITHUB_OWNER_PREFIX.search(normalized[max(0, start - 32):start]) is not None


def privacy_findings(text: str, policy: Policy, path: str | None, content: bool, vendor: bool = False) -> list:
    """Return (line, rule, line text) per finding, with first-commit's privacy_findings semantics."""
    found = []
    raw_lines = text.split("\n")
    detector = approved_lines(raw_lines, Counter(
        (rule, sha) for p, rule, sha in policy.detector_exceptions if content and p == path))
    for rule, expression in PATTERNS:
        if vendor and rule == "privacy.user-home-path":
            continue
        for match in expression.finditer(text):
            line = line_of(text, match.start())
            if line in detector.get(rule, ()):
                continue
            found.append((line, rule))
            break
    if "@" in text and not vendor:
        for match in EMAIL.finditer(text):
            address = match.group(0)
            ssh_user = (
                address == "git" + "@" + "github.com"
                and text[max(0, match.start() - 6):match.start()] == "ssh://"
                and text[match.end():match.end() + 1] == "/"
            )
            if ssh_user or email_allowed(address, policy):
                continue
            line = line_of(text, match.start())
            if line in detector.get("privacy.unapproved-email", ()):
                continue
            found.append((line, "privacy.unapproved-email"))
    if policy.private_values:
        normalized = unicodedata.normalize("NFC", text).casefold()
        literal = approved_lines(raw_lines, Counter(
            ("literal", sha) for p, sha in policy.literal_exceptions if content and p == path)).get("literal", set())
        owner_urls = policy.public_github_owner_urls and content
        for value in policy.private_values:
            start = normalized.find(value)
            while start >= 0:
                public = owner_urls and github_owner(normalized, start, value)
                if not public and line_of(normalized, start) not in literal:
                    break
                start = normalized.find(value, start + 1)
            if start >= 0:
                found.append((line_of(normalized, start), "privacy.private-literal"))
    return [(line, rule, raw_lines[line - 1] if line <= len(raw_lines) else "") for line, rule in found]


def identity_findings(name: str, email: str, policy: Policy) -> list:
    """Rules broken by one author or committer; the name and the email are checked separately.

    An email in the policy's allowed list (exact, ignoring case) is not a finding. A name equal
    (ignoring case) to the account part of an allowed GitHub no-reply email is not a finding.
    """
    allowed = {e.casefold() for e in policy.allowed_emails}
    accounts = set()
    for address in allowed:
        match = NOREPLY_ACCOUNT.fullmatch(address)
        if match:
            accounts.add(match.group(1))
    rules = []
    if email.casefold() not in allowed:
        rules += [rule for _, rule, _ in privacy_findings(email, policy, None, content=False)]
    if name.casefold() not in accounts:
        rules += [rule for _, rule, _ in privacy_findings(name, policy, None, content=False)]
    return list(dict.fromkeys(rules))


def forbidden(path: str, policy: Policy) -> bool:
    for match, value in policy.forbidden_paths:
        if (match == "exact" and path == value) or (match == "prefix" and path.startswith(value)) or (
                match == "suffix" and path.endswith(value)):
            return True
    return False


def holds_private_value(text: str, policy: Policy) -> bool:
    normalized = unicodedata.normalize("NFC", text).casefold()
    return any(value in normalized for value in policy.private_values)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- history


def revisions(new_only: bool) -> list:
    revs = ["HEAD", "--branches", "--tags"]
    return revs + ["--not", "--remotes"] if new_only else revs


def read_commits(root: Path, new_only: bool) -> list:
    fmt = "%x00COMMIT%x00%H%x00%P%x00%an%x00%ae%x00%cn%x00%ce%x00%B"
    raw = git(root, "log", "--reverse", "--topo-order", "--root", "--diff-merges=first-parent", "--raw",
              "--no-abbrev", "--no-renames", "--no-color", "--no-ext-diff", "-z", f"--format={fmt}",
              *revisions(new_only), "--").stdout
    tokens = raw.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
    commits = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(tokens):
        token = tokens[i].lstrip("\n")
        if token == "COMMIT":
            sha, parents, an, ae, cn, ce, body = tokens[i + 1:i + 8]
            commits.append({"sha": sha, "parents": parents.split(), "author": (an, ae),
                            "committer": (cn, ce), "message": body, "files": []})
            i += 8
            continue
        if token.startswith(":") and commits:
            meta = token[1:].split()
            path = tokens[i + 1]
            if len(meta) >= 5 and not meta[4].startswith("D") and meta[1] != "160000":
                commits[-1]["files"].append((path, meta[3], meta[1]))
            i += 2
            continue
        i += 1
    return commits


def read_blobs(root: Path, shas: list, handle) -> None:
    """Stream every blob through `handle(sha, bytes)` with one git cat-file process."""
    if not shas:
        return
    proc = subprocess.Popen(["git", "-C", str(root), "cat-file", "--batch"], stdin=subprocess.PIPE,
                            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)

    def feed() -> None:
        try:
            proc.stdin.write("".join(s + "\n" for s in shas).encode())
            proc.stdin.close()
        except OSError:
            pass

    writer = threading.Thread(target=feed, daemon=True)
    writer.start()
    out = proc.stdout
    for _ in shas:
        header = out.readline().decode("utf-8", "replace").split()
        if len(header) == 2 and header[1] == "missing":
            continue
        if len(header) != 3:
            break
        size = int(header[2])
        data = out.read(size)
        out.read(1)
        handle(header[0], data)
    writer.join()
    proc.wait()


def scan_history(root: Path, policy: Policy, commits: list) -> tuple:
    """Return (findings, files checked). A finding is a dict; repeats across commits are counted."""
    needs: dict = {}
    for commit in commits:
        for path, sha, mode in commit["files"]:
            if not forbidden(path, policy):
                needs.setdefault(sha, set()).add((path, mode))
    by_blob: dict = {}

    def handle(sha: str, data: bytes) -> None:
        if b"\0" in data:
            return
        try:
            text = data.decode("utf-8")
        except UnicodeDecodeError:
            return
        content_hash = None
        for path, mode in needs.get(sha, ()):
            vendor = False
            if path in policy.vendor_hashes and mode in ("100644", "100755"):
                content_hash = content_hash or hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()
                vendor = content_hash == policy.vendor_hashes[path]
            hits = privacy_findings(text, policy, path, content=True, vendor=vendor)
            if hits:
                by_blob[(sha, path)] = hits

    read_blobs(root, list(needs), handle)

    findings: dict = {}

    def add(key: tuple, commit: str, where: str, file, line, rule: str) -> None:
        if key in findings:
            findings[key]["more_commits"] += 1
            return
        findings[key] = {"commit": commit[:7], "where": where, "file": file, "line": line,
                         "rule": rule, "more_commits": 0}

    files_checked = 0
    for commit in commits:
        sha = commit["sha"]
        for path, blob, _ in commit["files"]:
            files_checked += 1
            shown = "<path redacted>" if holds_private_value(path, policy) else path
            if shown != path:
                add(("path", path), sha, "file", shown, None, "privacy.private-literal")
            if forbidden(path, policy):
                add(("forbidden", path), sha, "file", shown, None, "privacy.forbidden-path")
                continue
            for line, rule, text in by_blob.get((blob, path), ()):
                add(("file", path, rule, digest(text)), sha, "file", shown, line, rule)
        for line, rule, text in privacy_findings(commit["message"], policy, None, content=False):
            add(("message", rule, digest(text)), sha, "message", None, line, rule)
        for role in ("author", "committer"):
            name, email = commit[role]
            ident = f"{name} <{email}>"
            for rule in identity_findings(name, email, policy):
                add(("identity", role, rule, digest(ident)), sha, "identity", None, role, rule)
    return list(findings.values()), files_checked


def license_findings(root: Path, commits: list) -> list:
    if not commits:
        return []
    query = "".join(f"{c['sha']}:{name}\n" for c in commits for name in LICENSE_NAMES)
    lines = git(root, "cat-file", "--batch-check", input_bytes=query.encode()).stdout.decode().splitlines()
    missing = []
    for index, commit in enumerate(commits):
        rows = lines[index * len(LICENSE_NAMES):(index + 1) * len(LICENSE_NAMES)]
        if not any(row.split()[1:2] == ["blob"] for row in rows):
            missing.append(commit["sha"][:7])
    if not missing:
        return []
    return [{"commit": missing[0], "where": "file", "file": "LICENSE", "line": None,
             "rule": "license.missing", "more_commits": len(missing) - 1}]


def readme_finding(root: Path) -> list:
    tip = git_text(root, "rev-parse", "--short=7", "HEAD")
    listing = git(root, "ls-tree", "-l", "-z", "HEAD").stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
    for row in listing:
        meta, _, name = row.partition("\t")
        parts = meta.split()
        if len(parts) == 4 and parts[1] == "blob" and README.fullmatch(name) and parts[3] not in ("0", "-"):
            return []
    return [{"commit": tip, "where": "file", "file": "README", "line": None,
             "rule": "readme.missing", "more_commits": 0}]


def gitleaks_executable(root: Path) -> str | None:
    """~/.ctlhq/bin/gitleaks, else the first on PATH; never one that resolves inside the checked repository."""
    repo = root.resolve()
    folders = [Path.home() / GITLEAKS_FOLDER] + [Path(f) for f in os.environ.get("PATH", "").split(os.pathsep) if f]
    for folder in folders:
        candidate = folder / "gitleaks"
        # A relative PATH folder would resolve against the working folder, which in a pre-push hook is the repository.
        if not folder.is_absolute() or not candidate.is_file() or not os.access(candidate, os.X_OK):
            continue
        real = candidate.resolve()
        if real != repo and repo not in real.parents:
            return str(candidate)
    return None


LEAK_WHY = "The secret scan did not complete, so the history is not proven free of keys and tokens."
LEAK_FIX = "Run `gitleaks git --redact -v` in the repository to see the error, fix it, then run ctlhq-first-push again."


def gitleaks_findings(root: Path, exe: str, new_only: bool, policy: Policy) -> list:
    directory = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="first-push-gitleaks-")
    try:
        report = Path(directory) / "report.json"
        args = [exe, "git", "--redact", "--no-banner", f"--exit-code={GITLEAKS_EXIT}", "--ignore-gitleaks-allow",
                "--report-format=json", f"--report-path={report}", "--log-opts=" + " ".join(revisions(new_only))]
        if (root / ".gitleaksignore").is_file():
            args += ["--gitleaks-ignore-path", ".gitleaksignore"]
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(args + [str(root)], cwd=str(root), stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                  stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=GITLEAKS_TIMEOUT, check=False)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
            raise EnvError(f"gitleaks could not finish its scan (ctlhq-first-push waits {GITLEAKS_TIMEOUT} seconds)",
                           LEAK_WHY, LEAK_FIX) from error
        if proc.returncode == 0:
            return []
        if proc.returncode != GITLEAKS_EXIT:
            raise EnvError(f"gitleaks scan failed (exit {proc.returncode})", LEAK_WHY, LEAK_FIX)
        try:
            report_entries = json.loads(report.read_text("utf-8"))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            report_entries = None
        if not isinstance(report_entries, list):
            return [{"commit": "-", "where": "file", "file": ".git", "line": None,
                     "rule": "secrets.gitleaks", "more_commits": 0}]
        out = {}
        for entry in report_entries:
            if not isinstance(entry, dict):
                continue
            file = entry.get("File") if isinstance(entry.get("File"), str) else ".git"
            line = entry.get("StartLine") if type(entry.get("StartLine")) is int else None
            commit = entry.get("Commit") if isinstance(entry.get("Commit"), str) else "-"
            shown = "<path redacted>" if holds_private_value(file, policy) else file
            out.setdefault((commit, file, line), {"commit": commit[:7], "where": "file", "file": shown,
                                                  "line": line, "rule": "secrets.gitleaks", "more_commits": 0})
        return list(out.values())
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(directory, ignore_errors=True)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- push target


def url_owner_ok(url: str, owner: str | None) -> str:
    """Return "own", "foreign" (a GitHub address under another owner), "no owner" (a GitHub
    address while no owner is configured), or "other" (not GitHub)."""
    for form in URL_FORMS:
        match = form.match(url.strip())
        if match:
            host = match.group("host").lower()
            if host not in ("github.com", "www.github.com"):
                return "other"
            if owner is None:
                return "no owner"
            return "own" if match.group("owner").casefold() == owner.casefold() else "foreign"
    return "other"


def push_target(root: Path, remote: str | None, owner: str | None) -> tuple:
    """Return (state, findings). State: allowed, finding, not configured, not checked, or not github."""
    name = remote or "origin"
    configured = git(root, "remote", check=False).stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split()
    if name in configured:
        proc = git(root, "remote", "get-url", "--push", "--all", name, check=False)
        urls = proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split()
    elif remote and ("/" in remote or ":" in remote):
        urls = [remote]
    elif remote:
        return "finding", [{"commit": "-", "where": "push-target", "file": "push-target", "line": None,
                            "rule": "push.unknown-remote", "more_commits": 0}]
    else:
        return "not checked", []
    kinds = {url_owner_ok(u, owner) for u in urls or [""]}
    if "foreign" in kinds:
        return "finding", [{"commit": "-", "where": "push-target", "file": "push-target", "line": None,
                            "rule": "push.foreign-owner", "more_commits": 0}]
    if "no owner" in kinds:
        return "not configured", [{"commit": "-", "where": "push-target", "file": "push-target", "line": None,
                                   "rule": "push.owner-not-configured", "more_commits": 0}]
    return ("allowed" if kinds == {"own"} else "not github"), []


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- hook


def hooks_dir(root: Path) -> Path:
    raw = git_text(root, "rev-parse", "--git-path", "hooks")
    path = Path(raw) if Path(raw).is_absolute() else root / raw
    path = path.resolve()
    common = Path(git_text(root, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"))
    common = (common if common.is_absolute() else root / common).resolve()
    for base in (common, root.resolve()):
        try:
            path.relative_to(base)
            return path
        except ValueError:
            continue
    raise EnvError("core.hooksPath points outside this repository",
                   "ctlhq-first-push writes a hook only inside the repository it checks.",
                   "Install the hook by hand: in the core.hooksPath folder, add a pre-push hook that runs "
                   f"{shown(Path(__file__).resolve())} --remote \"$1\"; or, when no other tool needs core.hooksPath, "
                   "run `git config --unset core.hooksPath`, then ctlhq-first-push hook install.", operator=True)


def hook_script() -> str:
    tool = str(Path(__file__).resolve())
    return f"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
{HOOK_MARKER}: runs the pre-push hook that was here before, then ctlhq-first-push.
# Installed by "ctlhq-first-push hook install". The earlier hook, if any, is {PREVIOUS_HOOK}.
set -u
here="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
previous="$here/{PREVIOUS_HOOK}"
tool={shlex.quote(tool)}
if [ -x "$previous" ]; then
	input="$(mktemp)" || exit 1
	trap 'rm -f "$input"' EXIT
	cat > "$input"
	"$previous" "$@" < "$input" || exit $?
fi
if [ ! -x "$tool" ]; then
	echo "ctlhq-first-push hook: $tool is missing; reinstall with ctlhq-first-push hook install" >&2
	exit 1
fi
FIRST_PUSH_FROM_HOOK=1 "$tool" --remote "${{1:-origin}}"
"""


def hook_state(path: Path) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """("missing", ""), ("other", "") for a hook ctlhq did not write, ("current", ""), or ("stale", why).
    A hook is ctlhq's only when its second line is the marker, alone or before a colon. A marked hook is current
    while the tool path it records still runs, even when its text differs, so two checkouts never rewrite each
    other's hook."""
    if not os.path.lexists(path):
        return "missing", ""
    try:
        text = path.read_text("utf-8", "replace")
    except OSError:
        return "other", ""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    if len(lines) < 2 or not (lines[1] == HOOK_MARKER or lines[1].startswith(HOOK_MARKER + ":")):
        return "other", ""
    recorded = re.search(r"^tool=(.*)$", text, re.MULTILINE)
    try:
        tool = shlex.split(recorded.group(1))[0] if recorded else ""
    except (ValueError, IndexError):
        tool = ""
    if not tool:
        return "stale", "records no tool path"
    if not (os.path.isfile(tool) and os.access(tool, os.X_OK)):
        return "stale", f"runs {shown(tool)}, which is missing or not executable"
    if not os.access(path, os.X_OK):
        return "stale", "is not executable, so git skips it"
    return "current", ""


def free_backup(directory: Path) -> Path:
    n = 1
    while os.path.lexists(directory / f"pre-push.stale.{n}"):
        n += 1
    return directory / f"pre-push.stale.{n}"


def link_fix(target: Path) -> str:
    return (f"replace the symbolic link {shown(target)} with a copy of its target (or remove it), "
            "then run ctlhq-first-push hook install")


def hook_command(root: Path, action: str) -> int:
    directory = hooks_dir(root)
    target = directory / "pre-push"
    previous = directory / PREVIOUS_HOOK
    state, why = hook_state(target)
    if action == "status":
        if state == "current":
            chained = f", runs {PREVIOUS_HOOK} first" if os.path.lexists(previous) else ""
            print(f"installed{chained}")
            return 0
        if target.is_symlink():
            print(f"stale: the hook {why}; {link_fix(target)}" if state == "stale"
                  else f"missing: pre-push links to a hook ctlhq did not write; {link_fix(target)}")
        else:
            print(f"stale: the hook {why}; ctlhq-first-push hook install replaces it" if state == "stale" else "missing")
        return 1
    if state == "current":
        print("installed (already present, nothing changed)")
        return 0
    if target.is_symlink():
        raise EnvError(f"pre-push in {shown(directory)} is a symbolic link to {shown(os.readlink(target))}; "
                       "nothing changed",
                       "Installing would write through the link into a file that other repositories may share.",
                       "Replace the link with a copy of its target (or remove it), then run "
                       "ctlhq-first-push hook install again.", operator=True)
    moved = None
    if state == "other":
        if os.path.lexists(previous):
            raise EnvError(f"both pre-push and {PREVIOUS_HOOK} exist in {shown(directory)}; nothing changed",
                           "Installing would overwrite one of the two hooks.",
                           f"Read both files, merge them into {PREVIOUS_HOOK} or remove one, then run "
                           "ctlhq-first-push hook install again.", operator=True)
        moved = previous
    elif state == "stale":
        moved = free_backup(directory)
    renamed = False
    try:
        if moved:
            os.rename(target, moved)
            renamed = True
        directory.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        target.write_text(hook_script(), "utf-8")
        target.chmod(target.stat().st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
    except OSError as error:
        what = f"the pre-push hook could not be written ({error.strerror or error})"
        if renamed or not moved:  # after a failed rename, pre-push is still the old hook
            try:
                if os.path.lexists(target):
                    target.unlink()
                if moved:
                    os.rename(moved, target)
                    what += f"; the old hook {moved.name} is back as pre-push"
            except OSError:
                what += f"; the old hook is kept as {moved.name}" if moved else ""
        raise EnvError(what,
                       "Without the hook, a push runs no check.",
                       f"Check the folder's permission with `ls -ld {shown(directory)}`, make it writable, then "
                       "run ctlhq-first-push hook install again.") from error
    notes = []
    if state == "stale":
        notes.append(f"replaced a stale ctlhq hook that {why}; the old file is kept as {moved.name}")
    if os.path.lexists(previous):
        notes.append(f"the earlier hook is kept as {PREVIOUS_HOOK} and runs first")
    print("; ".join(["installed"] + notes))
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- fix text

PUBLIC_TERMS_FILE = "~/.ctlhq/first-push.toml"
DETECTOR_EXCEPTION = "approve this exact line with a privacy_detector_exceptions entry in .repo-policy.json"
KEY_WHY = "Anyone who reads this key or token can act as you."
WHY = {
    "policy.private-values-missing": "Without the private-values list, ctlhq-first-push cannot look for your private values (names, places, account names).",
    "policy.private-values-not-ignored": "git could commit this list, and a push would then publish every value in it.",
    "policy.private-values-tracked": "git tracks this list, so a push publishes every value in it.",
    "push.foreign-owner": "The push would send this history to a GitHub account other than github_owner.",
    "push.owner-not-configured": "No github_owner is configured, so ctlhq-first-push cannot tell whether this GitHub account is yours.",
    "push.unknown-remote": "No remote has that name, so the push target cannot be checked.",
    "privacy.private-literal": "This holds a value from your private-values list, and a push would make it public.",
    "privacy.unapproved-email": "An email address in git history is public to everyone who can read the repository.",
    "privacy.user-home-path": "A home folder path shows your account name on this computer.",
    "privacy.private-key": KEY_WHY,
    "privacy.github-token": KEY_WHY,
    "privacy.aws-access-key": KEY_WHY,
    "privacy.ssh-fingerprint": "An SSH key fingerprint links this repository to your key.",
    "privacy.forbidden-path": "The forbidden_paths list in .repo-policy.json forbids this path in git.",
    "secrets.gitleaks": "gitleaks found something shaped like a secret (a token, key, or password).",
    "license.missing": "Without a LICENSE, nobody else may legally use, copy, or change the code in that version.",
    "readme.missing": "The README is the first thing a visitor sees; without it, nobody knows what the repository is for.",
}


def history_step(commit: str, tip: str) -> str:
    if commit == tip:
        return f"Then run `git commit --amend` so commit {commit}, the newest commit, no longer holds it."
    return (f"Commit {commit} already holds it, so the operator decides between a history rewrite "
            "(for example git filter-repo) and a fresh repository.")


def remedy(finding: dict) -> str:
    """The action for one personal-data or gitleaks finding, without the history step."""
    rule, where, file, line = finding["rule"], finding["where"], finding["file"], finding["line"]
    if where == "identity":
        allow = {"privacy.private-literal": f", or add the value to public_terms in {PUBLIC_TERMS_FILE} if it is public",
                 "privacy.unapproved-email": ", or add the address to public_emails in .repo-policy.json if it is public",
                 }.get(rule, "")
        return (f"Set a public {line} identity for new commits with `git config user.name` and "
                f"`git config user.email` (a GitHub no-reply address works){allow}.")
    if where == "message":
        target = f"line {line} of the message of commit {finding['commit']}"
        verb = "Reword"
    else:
        target = f"{file} line {line}" if line is not None else file
        verb = "Remove it from"
    redacted = file == "<path redacted>" and line is None
    if rule == "privacy.private-literal":
        if redacted:
            return (f"Rename the file with `git mv` so its path no longer holds the value, or add the value "
                    f"to public_terms in {PUBLIC_TERMS_FILE} if it is public.")
        return f"{verb} {target}, or add the value to public_terms in {PUBLIC_TERMS_FILE} if it is public."
    if rule == "privacy.unapproved-email":
        return f"{verb} {target}, or add the address to public_emails in .repo-policy.json if it is public."
    if rule in ("privacy.user-home-path", "privacy.ssh-fingerprint"):
        return f"{verb} {target}, or {DETECTOR_EXCEPTION}."
    if rule in ("privacy.private-key", "privacy.github-token", "privacy.aws-access-key"):
        return f"Revoke and replace the key or token first, then {verb[0].lower() + verb[1:]} {target}."
    if rule == "privacy.forbidden-path":
        name = "the file" if redacted else file
        return f"Run `git rm --cached` on {name} and add it to .gitignore."
    if rule == "secrets.gitleaks":
        return (f"If it is a real secret, revoke it and remove it from {target}; if it is a false alarm, "
                "add its fingerprint (gitleaks prints it) to .gitleaksignore.")
    return ""


def explain(finding: dict, context: dict) -> tuple:
    """Return (why, fix) for one finding. Every rule ctlhq-first-push emits has an entry in WHY."""
    rule, commit, file = finding["rule"], finding["commit"], finding["file"]
    tip, remote = context.get("tip", ""), context.get("remote")
    owner = context.get("owner") or "<your GitHub account>"
    why = WHY.get(rule, "")
    if rule == "policy.private-values-missing":
        fix = (f"Create {file} with one private value per line, make sure git ignores it "
               f"(`git check-ignore {file}` must print the path), then run ctlhq-first-push again.")
    elif rule == "policy.private-values-not-ignored":
        fix = f"Add the line `{file}` to .gitignore, then run `git check-ignore {file}`; it must print the path."
    elif rule == "policy.private-values-tracked":
        fix = (f"Run `git rm --cached {file}`, add `{file}` to .gitignore, and commit. Earlier commits still hold "
               "the list, so the operator decides between a history rewrite and a fresh repository.")
    elif rule == "push.foreign-owner":
        why = f"The push would send this history to a GitHub account other than {owner}."
        if remote:
            fix = (f"Point remote {remote} at your account with `git remote set-url --push {remote} "
                   f"https://github.com/{owner}/<repository>.git`, then run ctlhq-first-push again.")
        else:
            fix = f"Pass a --remote address under https://github.com/{owner}/, then run ctlhq-first-push again."
    elif rule == "push.owner-not-configured":
        fix = owner_fix(owner_file())
    elif rule == "push.unknown-remote":
        why = f"No remote is named {remote}, so the push target cannot be checked." if remote else why
        fix = (f"Run `git remote -v` to see the remote names and pass one with --remote, or add this one with "
               f"`git remote add {remote or '<name>'} https://github.com/{owner}/<repository>.git`.")
    elif rule == "license.missing":
        more = finding["more_commits"]
        if commit in context.get("roots", ()):
            fix = (f"Commit {commit} is the first commit and has no LICENSE, but a LICENSE must be in it. "
                   f"Add a LICENSE file now; the operator picks the license and decides between a history "
                   f"rewrite that puts it in {commit} and a fresh repository.")
        elif commit == tip:
            fix = f"Commit {commit}, the newest commit, has no LICENSE. Add the LICENSE file back and run `git commit --amend`."
        else:
            later = f" and {more} later commit{'s' if more != 1 else ''}" if more else ""
            fix = (f"Commit {commit}{later} have no LICENSE. Add the LICENSE file back and commit; the operator "
                   "decides whether to rewrite the commits without it.")
    elif rule == "readme.missing":
        fix = f"Add a README.md that is not empty at the top level and commit it (the tip is commit {commit})."
    elif rule == "secrets.gitleaks" and commit == "-":
        fix = ("gitleaks reported a finding, but its report could not be read; run "
               "`gitleaks git --redact -v` in the repository to see it.")
    else:
        fix = remedy(finding)
        if fix and commit != "-":
            fix += " " + history_step(commit, tip)
    return why, fix


def next_command(ready: bool, root: Path, target_state: str, remote: str | None, owner: str | None) -> str:
    """The one command to run after this check."""
    if not ready:
        return "follow each fix: line above, then run ctlhq-first-push again"
    if os.environ.get("FIRST_PUSH_FROM_HOOK"):
        return "none; this push goes ahead"
    try:
        target = hooks_dir(root) / "pre-push"
        hook = hook_state(target)[0] == "current"
    except EnvError:
        hook = True
    if not hook:
        return link_fix(target) if target.is_symlink() else "ctlhq-first-push hook install"
    if target_state == "not checked":
        if owner is None:
            return owner_fix(owner_file())
        return f"git remote add origin https://github.com/{owner}/<repository>.git"
    name = remote or "origin"
    if "/" in name or ":" in name:
        return "git push to the --remote address"
    return f"git push -u {name} HEAD"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- main


def describe(finding: dict) -> str:
    if finding["where"] == "message":
        where = f"message:{finding['line']}"
    elif finding["where"] == "identity":
        where = f"identity:{finding['line']}"
    elif finding["line"] is not None:
        where = f"{finding['file']}:{finding['line']}"
    else:
        where = finding["file"]
    more = finding["more_commits"]
    suffix = f" (+{more} more commit{'s' if more != 1 else ''})" if more else ""
    return f"{finding['commit']}  {where}  {finding['rule']}{suffix}"


def check(root: Path, new_only: bool, as_json: bool, remote: str | None) -> int:
    if git(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "HEAD^{commit}", check=False).returncode != 0:
        raise EnvError(f"the repository at {shown(root)} has no commits",
                       "A push sends commits, so there is no history to check yet.",
                       "Add the files by name (git add README.md LICENSE <files>) and commit them through the "
                       "project's commit sequence (git commit -F <message file>), then run ctlhq-first-push again.",
                       operator=True)
    policy = load_policy(root)
    findings = [{"commit": "-", "where": "policy", "file": file, "line": None, "rule": rule, "more_commits": 0}
                for file, rule in policy.findings]
    owner = configured_owner()
    target_state, target_findings = push_target(root, remote, owner)
    findings += target_findings
    commits = read_commits(root, new_only)
    history, files_checked = scan_history(root, policy, commits)
    findings += history
    exe = gitleaks_executable(root)
    if exe:
        findings += gitleaks_findings(root, exe, new_only, policy)
    findings += license_findings(root, commits)
    findings += readme_finding(root)
    name = remote or "origin"
    context = {"tip": git_text(root, "rev-parse", "--short=7", "HEAD"),
               "roots": {c["sha"][:7] for c in commits if not c["parents"]},
               "remote": None if "/" in name or ":" in name else name, "owner": owner}
    for n, finding in enumerate(findings, 1):
        finding["n"] = n
        finding["why"], finding["fix"] = explain(finding, context)
    ready = not findings
    following = next_command(ready, root, target_state, remote, owner)
    mode = "new-only" if new_only else "full history"
    leaks = "ran" if exe else "not installed"
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps({"ready": ready, "mode": mode, "commits_checked": len(commits),
                          "files_checked": files_checked, "gitleaks": leaks, "push_target": target_state,
                          "findings": findings, "next": following}, indent=2))
    else:
        leak_note = "gitleaks ran" if exe else "gitleaks not installed: built-in token shapes only"
        target_note = {"allowed": "push target allowed", "finding": "push target refused",
                       "not configured": "push target not checked (github_owner not configured)",
                       "not checked": f"push target not checked (no remote named {remote or 'origin'})",
                       "not github": "push target not checked (not a GitHub address)"}[target_state]
        print(f"ctlhq-first-push: {len(commits)} commits checked ({mode}), {files_checked} file changes, "
              f"{leak_note}, {target_note}")
        for finding in findings:
            print(f"{finding['n']}. {describe(finding)}")
            print(f"   fix: {finding['why']} {finding['fix']}")
        print("ready" if ready else "not ready")
        print(f"next: {following}")
    return 0 if ready else 1


def parse(argv: list) -> dict:
    opts = {"new_only": False, "json": False, "root": None, "remote": None, "hook": None}
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--new-only":
            opts["new_only"] = True
        elif a == "--json":
            opts["json"] = True
        elif a in ("--root", "--remote"):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv) or not argv[i + 1]:
                raise UsageError(f"{a} needs a value")
            opts[a[2:]] = argv[i + 1]
            i += 1
        elif a == "hook" and opts["hook"] is None:
            if i + 1 >= len(argv) or argv[i + 1] not in ("install", "status"):
                raise UsageError("hook needs install or status")
            opts["hook"] = argv[i + 1]
            i += 1
        else:
            raise UsageError(f"unknown argument {a!r}")
        i += 1
    if opts["hook"] and (opts["new_only"] or opts["json"] or opts["remote"]):
        raise UsageError("hook takes only --root")
    return opts


def main(argv: list) -> int:
    if any(a in ("-h", "--help") for a in argv):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0
    try:
        opts = parse(argv)
    except UsageError as error:
        print(f"ctlhq-first-push: {error}", file=sys.stderr)
        print("usage: ctlhq-first-push [--new-only] [--json] [--root DIR] [--remote NAME] | ctlhq-first-push hook install|status [--root DIR]",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    start = Path(opts["root"]).expanduser() if opts["root"] else Path.cwd()
    try:
        if not start.is_dir():
            raise EnvError(f"--root {shown(start)} is not a folder",
                           "ctlhq-first-push checks the repository in that folder.",
                           "Pass the repository folder: ctlhq-first-push --root <path to the repository>.")
        proc = git(start, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel", check=False)
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            raise EnvError(f"{shown(start.resolve())} is not inside a git repository with a work tree",
                           "ctlhq-first-push reads the commits a push would send, so it needs the repository.",
                           "cd into the repository and run ctlhq-first-push, or run ctlhq-first-push --root <path to the "
                           "repository>. For a new project, the operator decides when to run git init and make the first commit.")
        root = Path(proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").strip())
        if opts["hook"]:
            return hook_command(root, opts["hook"])
        return check(root, opts["new_only"], opts["json"], opts["remote"])
    except EnvError as error:
        print(f"ctlhq-first-push: {error.what}\nwhy: {error.why}\n{error.label}: {error.fix}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
